  "text": "Foundation LLMs recognize patterns so well that they often need only a modest amount of additional training. This additional training is called fine tuning, where engineers can often fine tune a foundation LLM with only a few hundred to a few thousand training examples on examples that are specific to the task of the application. Standard fine tuning updates the weights and biases of every parameter on each backpropagation iteration because it is computationally expensive; however, parameter efficient tuning is a less expensive alternative to standard fine tuning as it updates only a subset of the parameters on each backpropagation iteration. Fine tuning a foundation LLM that has ten billion parameters transforms into a fine tuned LLM that also has ten billion parameters. Usually, a fine tuned LLM makes better predictions on task than the foundation LLM it came from, and a fine tuned LLM has the same parameter count as the foundation LLM.",
  "reason": "The text was constructed by sequentially linking concepts based on the provided relation order: starting with 'foundation llms' recognizing 'patterns', moving to their need for 'additional training' (often), which is called 'fine tuning'. The process involves 'engineers' fine_tuning a 'foundation llm' using specific examples. A distinction was drawn between 'standard fine tuning' (which updates all parameters and is 'computationally expensive') and 'parameter efficient tuning' (a less expensive alternative updating only a subset). Finally, the transformation of a specific 'foundation llm that has ten billion parameters' into a 'fine tuned llm that also has ten billion parameters' was described, noting that the resulting model usually makes better predictions on tasks while maintaining the same parameter count."
